{
  "title": "PrestaShop \u2014 ecommerce (9,080\u2605)",
  "content": "Project: PrestaShop\nDomain: ecommerce | Scale: large | Stars: 9,080\nProblem: PrestaShop provides an open-source e-commerce platform that enables merchants to build, manage, and customize fully-featured online stores without vendor lock-in. It addresses the need for a flexible, multilingual, multi-currency shopping solution suitable for businesses of all sizes.\nTarget users: Online merchants and retailers, E-commerce developers and agencies, Small to medium-sized businesses, Enterprise e-commerce operators, Theme and module developers\nCore features: Product catalog and inventory management; Shopping cart and checkout experience; Multi-language and multi-currency support with localization; Back-office administration panel for store management; Module and theme system for extensibility and customization; Payment service integrations; Responsive front and back office design; Docker-based development environment\nIntegrations: MySQL / MariaDB / Percona Server, Apache / Nginx web servers, phpMyAdmin, Docker / Docker Compose, Multiple payment gateways (generic), Maildev (email testing)\nTech stack: TypeScript, JavaScript, SCSS, Twig, Smarty, HTML + PHP 8.1+ + MySQL 5.6+ (MySQL, MariaDB, Percona Server) + Docker, Apache/Nginx\nKey libraries: Symfony (PHP framework), Twig (templating), Smarty (legacy templating), Composer (PHP dependency management), Playwright/Gherkin (UI/integration testing)\nArchitecture: monolith\nDesign lessons:\n- Dual templating engine strategy (Twig for modern back-office, Smarty for legacy front-office) reflects a long-term migration path in a mature codebase.\n- Extensive use of Gherkin-based BDD tests alongside PHP unit and UI tests indicates a strong investment in multi-layer quality assurance for a complex platform.\n- Module and hook-based extensibility pattern allows third-party developers to customize behavior without forking core, a critical design decision for an open-source CMS/e-commerce platform.\n- Branching strategy separating stable releases from a develop branch (with version-specific focus) signals disciplined release management needed for a widely-deployed production platform.",
